\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Background}

Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems combine information retrieval with language generation. This design solves a problem with purely parametric large language models (LLMs): a model's knowledge is fixed at training time and cannot be updated without retraining or fine-tuning it. \citet{lewis2020rag} introduced RAG to address this problem by combining a pretrained retriever with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, so the model can base its output on external, updatable evidence instead of only on what it learned during training. In a RAG system, a user query is first used to retrieve relevant information from an external knowledge base, typically a vector database. This retrieved information is then given to a language model, together with instructions for generating the final response.

The system prompt connects the intended behavior of the system to its observable output. Unlike the retrieved evidence, which depends on the query and the knowledge base, the system prompt is written on purpose: it specifies how the model should behave, which instructions it should follow, which constraints it should respect, and which output format it should produce. Because the system prompt is written in natural language rather than a formal specification, its effect on behavior is hard to predict and hard to check in advance. This is why its influence on both intended behavior and actual output needs to be studied through controlled experiments.

\section{Motivation and Research Objective}

Understanding how a RAG system responds to its system prompt matters in practice. Whether a system reliably follows its instructions decides whether its behavior can be trusted once deployed, especially when the same retrieval and generation components are reused across many applications with only the system prompt changing. This raises the question of \textit{how systematic changes in the system prompt can be shown to alter the behavior and outcomes of a RAG system.}

At the same time, isolating the effect of the system prompt is hard. Its influence overlaps with the influence of the retrieved evidence, the language model itself, and even the exact wording of the instructions. All of these must be controlled before any change in behavior can be attributed to the system prompt rather than to something else. Prior work on prompt sensitivity in language models has shown that even small, seemingly harmless changes in wording can cause large changes in model behavior (e.g., Salinas and Morstatter 2024; Mu et al. 2025), and that this sensitivity remains even under adversarial or conflicting user input. This is why the present thesis treats the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable, not just a minor setting.

This thesis therefore focuses on one central research question: \textit{To what extent do systematic changes in the system prompt alter the behavior and outcomes of a RAG system when the user input, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and underlying language model are held constant?} Answering it requires two things: a formal model of the RAG system precise enough to isolate the system prompt as a controlled parameter, and an experimental design that holds every other source of variation fixed. The rest of this thesis builds both.

\section{Thesis Structure}

This thesis has seven chapters. Chapter 2 covers the background needed for the rest of the work: it traces how retrieval-augmented approaches to language generation developed, and introduces the components of a RAG system used throughout later chapters. Chapter 3 reviews the relevant literature in three parts, prompt sensitivity in language models, retrieval-augmented generation specifically, and adversarial instructions delivered through retrieved content, and identifies the resulting research gap. Chapter 4 builds a formal model of the RAG system used in this thesis: the probabilistic formulation of \citet{lewis2020rag}, extended so that the system prompt becomes an explicit variable of the generator, together with a formalization of the documents to be retrieved and a definition of what counts as a good answer. Chapter 5 describes the experimental setup: how the corpus and query set are generated, and which estimators are used for the criterion defined in Chapter 4. Chapter 6 presents and discusses the results of the experiments. Chapter 7 concludes the thesis by summarizing the findings and outlining directions for future work.

\section*{Notation}

Throughout this thesis, the following notation is used:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $q \in \mathcal{Q}$ denotes a user query, drawn from the space of possible queries $\mathcal{Q}$.
    \item $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\} \in \mathcal{D}$ denotes the knowledge base, where each $d_i$ is a document, $N$ is the number of documents, and $\mathcal{D}$ is the space of possible knowledge bases.
    \item $S \in \mathcal{S}$ denotes a system prompt, drawn from the space of possible system prompts $\mathcal{S}$.
    \item $C \in \mathcal{C}$ denotes the retrieved context supplied to the language model.
    \item $y \in \mathcal{Y}$ denotes the generated system output, drawn from the space of possible responses $\mathcal{Y}$.
    \item $z \in D$ denotes a single retrieved document, and $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ the $K$ documents with the highest retrieval probability.
    \item $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$ denotes the retrieval distribution with parameters $\eta$, and $p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S)$ the generator with parameters $\theta$.
    \item $Y$ denotes the output viewed as a random variable, and $M(Y)$ a measured property of it.
    \item $\Sigma = \{\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m\}$ denotes the set of requirements a system prompt states, where each $\sigma_k$ is a property an answer either has or does not have.
    \item $\varphi$ denotes the wording in which those requirements are expressed, and $S = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi)$ denotes the resulting system prompt.
\end{itemize}

\chapter{Preliminaries}

Chapter 1 established that the system prompt is the interface through which a RAG system's intended behavior is specified, and asked how systematically varying this interface changes system behavior and outcomes. Before that question can be answered formally, the components of a RAG system and the reasons behind its design need to be introduced. This chapter covers that background: Section 2.1 traces how retrieval-augmented approaches to language generation developed, Section 2.2 introduces the components of a RAG system used throughout later chapters, and Section 2.3 discusses typical use cases and the failure modes they expose.

\section{Path towards RAG}

The need to store knowledge outside of any single memory, and to retrieve it again on demand, predates computing by a wide margin. Inca administrators recorded census and tax data in knotted cords, quipu, that only specially trained officials could encode or decode, separating the record itself from the person consulting it. Centuries earlier, the Library of Alexandria's cataloger Callimachus organized its holdings by author and subject in the Pinakes, one of the first attempts to make a large external collection searchable rather than merely stored. \citet{bush1945asmaythink} made the need for machine-assisted retrieval explicit, proposing a device that could store a person's library and retrieve from it through associative links between related items rather than a single fixed order. \citet{codd1970relational} gave this idea a rigorous computational form, treating a knowledge store as a formally queryable structure, and \citet{salton1975vectorspace} extended retrieval from exact structured queries to similarity ranking over unstructured text, the direct ancestor of the embedding-based retrieval described later in this chapter.

This same need, a mechanism to retrieve from an external store rather than to remember everything internally, carried directly into question-answering systems, which did not begin with language models either. Early systems relied on hand-crafted rules and pattern matching over the input question, mapping recognized syntactic patterns directly onto retrieval or answer-generation procedures \citep{pandya2021qasurvey}. As statistical and later neural methods replaced these rule-based components, the dominant approach shifted toward learning the mapping from question to answer directly from data, culminating in large language models that generate answers from parameters learned over large text corpora rather than from any explicit rule set.

Large language models generate text using only parametric knowledge, that is, information encoded in their weights during training \citep{wang2024knowledgemechanisms}. This has two problems. First, a model's knowledge is fixed at the point its training data was collected: any event, fact, or document from after that point is unknown to the model unless it is retrained or fine-tuned, which is costly and has to be repeated every time knowledge changes. Second, purely parametric models are prone to hallucination: they can generate fluent, plausible-sounding statements that no evidence actually supports, because nothing in their design forces an output to trace back to a verifiable source.

Both problems are the reason to give a language model access to an external, updatable source of evidence at inference time, instead of relying only on what is stored in its parameters \citep{lin2025llminference}. \citet{lewis2020rag} formalized this idea as Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It combines a pretrained neural retriever, which selects relevant passages from a document collection, with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, which conditions its output on those retrieved passages in addition to the input query. This means the same language model can be paired with different or updated document collections without retraining, and a generated response can be grounded in, and in principle traced back to, specific retrieved evidence.

Since it was introduced, RAG has developed beyond this original design. \citet{gao2023survey} describe three stages of this development: Naive RAG, where retrieval and generation run as one fixed pipeline; Advanced RAG, which adds steps such as query reformulation or re-ranking retrieved passages before generation; and Modular RAG, where retrieval, generation, and other steps are separate, swappable components that can be rearranged for different tasks. In all of these variants, retrieval and generation stay separate steps. This is why this thesis treats retrieval and generation as two distinct functions, so their interaction with the system prompt can be studied on its own, as formalized in Chapter 4.

\section{Components of RAG}

A RAG system has four components: the knowledge base that evidence is retrieved from, the retriever that selects relevant evidence for a given query, the generator that produces a response from the query and the retrieved evidence, and the system prompt that specifies how the generator should behave. This section introduces each component in turn; Chapter 4 treats them formally. Figure~\ref{fig:rag} shows how they fit together and which data passes between them.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{\input{figures/rag_architecture.tex}}
\caption{Architecture of a RAG system, showing the four components introduced in this section. The retrieval stage matches the user query against a vector database and returns the top-$K$ chunks. The generation stage combines those chunks with the user query and the system prompt into a single prompt and produces the answer.}
\label{fig:rag}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Knowledge Base / Vector Database}

The knowledge base $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\}$ is the collection of documents that evidence is retrieved from. In most modern RAG systems, documents are first split into smaller passages, called chunks. This is needed because a language model can only take in a limited amount of context, and retrieving a whole document would add mostly irrelevant text along with the relevant part. The chunk size is a trade-off: small chunks are precise but may separate a statement from the sentence that qualifies it, while large chunks keep more context but dilute the part that actually matches the query. \citet{chen2024densex} compare retrieval units of different granularity and report that the choice changes both retrieval quality and the quality of the final answer, which makes chunk size a design parameter rather than an implementation detail.

Each chunk is then mapped to a high-dimensional embedding vector by an embedding model, so that chunks with similar meaning end up close together in the embedding space. Representing text as vectors and measuring relatedness by geometric proximity goes back to the vector space model of \citet{salton1975vectorspace}; what changed with neural embeddings is that the vector is learned rather than derived from term counts. Similarity between a query vector and a chunk vector is usually measured by the cosine of the angle between them, which ignores vector length and compares direction only.

These embeddings are stored in a vector database, which supports fast nearest-neighbor search over large collections of chunks. Comparing a query against every stored vector is exact but becomes too slow as the collection grows, so vector databases index the vectors and use approximate nearest-neighbor search, which trades a small loss in recall for a large gain in speed. The most widely used index of this kind is the hierarchical navigable small world graph of \citet{malkov2020hnsw}, which reaches a nearest neighbour by descending through graph layers of decreasing coarseness instead of scanning the collection. Several systems build on such indexes, and \citet{pan2024vectordb} survey how they differ in indexing, query processing and storage; for this section the relevant difference is how they are deployed. FAISS is a library that is embedded directly in an application and gives fine-grained control over the index structure \citep{johnson2021faiss}. Chroma is a lightweight database aimed at local development and small deployments. The \texttt{pgvector} extension adds vector columns and similarity operators to PostgreSQL, which allows vectors to sit next to existing relational data. Pinecone is a managed service in which indexing and scaling are handled by the provider. For the purposes of this thesis the relevant point is that all four answer the same question, namely which stored vectors lie closest to a query vector, so the choice affects latency, operational cost and reproducibility rather than the logic of the pipeline. The indexing step as a whole, in particular the choice of chunk size and embedding model, decides what evidence is even available to the retriever, regardless of how good the retriever itself is.

\subsection{Retriever}

Given a user query $q$ and a knowledge base $D$, the retriever selects the chunks that are relevant for answering $q$. Chapter 4 formalizes this as a probability distribution $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$ over candidate documents. Three approaches are common, and they fail in different ways.

\subsubsection{Dense Retrieval}

Dense retrieval embeds the query with the same embedding model used for the knowledge base and ranks chunks by the similarity between the query embedding and each chunk embedding. Because the embedding is learned, two passages that express the same content in different words are placed close together, so a query can match a chunk with which it shares no vocabulary. This is the mechanism the original RAG architecture of \citet{lewis2020rag} relies on. The weakness is the mirror image of the strength: rare identifiers, product codes, names and numbers carry little semantic signal, and a dense retriever may rank a fluent but irrelevant passage above the one chunk that contains the exact string the query asked about.

\subsubsection{Sparse Retrieval}

Sparse retrieval scores chunks by term overlap with the query. The standard method is BM25 \citep{robertson2009bm25}, which weights each matching term by how often it occurs in the chunk, how rare it is across the collection, and how long the chunk is, so that common words contribute little and long chunks are not favoured merely for containing more words. The representation is sparse because a chunk is described by the vocabulary it actually contains rather than by a dense vector. BM25 requires no training and remains a strong baseline, and it handles exactly the cases dense retrieval struggles with, since an exact term match is what it scores. It fails on paraphrase: a query and a chunk that share no terms receive a score of zero regardless of how closely their meanings agree.

\subsubsection{Hybrid Search}

Since the two methods fail on different queries, many systems run both and combine the results. The difficulty is that the two scores are not on a common scale, so they cannot simply be added. The usual solutions are to normalise the scores before combining them with a weight, or to combine the two rankings rather than the scores. Reciprocal rank fusion \citep{cormack2009rrf} is the standard method of the second kind: each chunk is scored by the reciprocal of its rank in each list, so a chunk ranked highly by either method is promoted without the two scores ever being compared directly. \citet{gao2023survey} describe hybrid retrieval as one of the standard refinements distinguishing later RAG systems from the original design.

\subsubsection{Retrieval Depth}

Whichever method is used, the top-$K$ ranked chunks are returned as the retrieved context. Retrieval quality depends on both how many chunks are retrieved and how precise the ranking is: too few chunks risk missing needed evidence, while too many risk adding irrelevant or conflicting information to the generation step. The order of the chunks also matters, since \citet{liu2024lostmiddle} report that answer accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits within the context. This thesis fixes the retriever, the chunking and $K$ across all experimental conditions, so that changes in the output cannot be traced back to retrieval.

\subsection{Generator}
\label{sec:generator}

The generator is the large language model that produces the final response. A large language model is a neural network trained on a large text corpus to predict the next token in a sequence, and it generates text by autoregressive decoding: producing one token at a time, each conditioned on all previous tokens. Its parameters, written $\theta$ in Chapter 4, encode what the model learned during training. This is often called parametric memory, and it is fixed once training ends.

Within a RAG pipeline the model is not used primarily as a store of facts. It is used as a reasoning and synthesis component that is conditioned on evidence supplied at inference time: it reads the retrieved chunks, selects the parts that answer the query, resolves references between them, and writes the result as fluent text. This division is the point of the architecture. Facts come from the knowledge base, which can be updated without retraining, while the model contributes language competence and the ability to combine several passages into one answer \citep{lewis2020rag}. When the model answers from parametric memory instead of from the retrieved context, the answer may still be fluent and even correct, but it is no longer grounded in evidence that can be checked, which is one route to the unsupported claims documented by \citet{niu2024ragtruth}.

In practice the query, the retrieved context and the system prompt are not passed to the model separately. They are combined, following a fixed template, into a single input sequence, usually with the system prompt placed first so that it shapes how everything after it is read. Chapter 4 formalizes the generator as $p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S)$, the probability of the next token given the query, a retrieved document, the tokens already produced and the system prompt. Because the query, context and system prompt together determine the model's input, changing any one of them can change the output, which is why the others must be held fixed if a change in behaviour is to be attributed to the system prompt alone.

\subsubsection{Decoding and the Temperature Parameter}

Generation is not deterministic by default. At each step the model produces a score $z_v$ for every token $v$ in the vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, and these scores are turned into a probability distribution by the softmax function with a temperature parameter $T > 0$:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:temperature}
p(v) = \frac{\exp(z_v / T)}{\sum_{v' \in \mathcal{V}} \exp(z_{v'} / T)}.
\end{equation}

The temperature rescales the scores before they are normalised, and it controls how much probability mass is left for tokens the model considers less likely \citep{holtzman2020degeneration}. As $T$ approaches zero the distribution concentrates on the highest-scoring token, and decoding becomes effectively deterministic: the same input produces the same output. As $T$ grows the distribution flattens, lower-scoring tokens become reachable, and the output becomes more varied across runs.

For open-ended writing this variety is useful. For a RAG system it is mostly a liability. A token that the model scores as unlikely given the retrieved context is, by construction, one that the evidence does not support well, so raising the temperature raises the chance of producing a statement the retrieved documents do not contain. High temperature also makes the system harder to evaluate, since the same query and the same evidence no longer yield the same answer. RAG systems intended to be faithful to their sources are therefore usually run at a low temperature, trading variety for grounding and reproducibility. This thesis holds $T$ fixed and low across all experimental conditions; the residual randomness that remains at a fixed temperature is the reason each condition is repeated several times in Chapter 5.

\subsection{System Prompt}

The system prompt $S$ specifies the instructions, constraints and output requirements the generator should follow, independent of the specific query or retrieved context. Unlike the query, which changes with every request, and the retrieved context, which changes with the query and the knowledge base, the system prompt is usually fixed across many requests.

Its role in the architecture is easiest to see by asking what happens without it. Retrieval places relevant evidence in the context window, but placing evidence there does not oblige the model to use it. Section~\ref{sec:generator} described the parameters $\theta$ as a store of information absorbed during training, and that store remains available at inference time. A model given a retrieved passage and a question it believes it already knows the answer to may answer from parameters rather than from the passage, producing an assured and fluent response that the retrieved documents do not support. This failure is not signalled to the user, since fluency and confidence are unaffected by whether the underlying claim was grounded; \citet{niu2024ragtruth} document that unsupported statements continue to appear even when relevant evidence has been retrieved and supplied. The system prompt is the last point in the pipeline at which this can be constrained. It is the only component that can state, before generation begins, that the retrieved context is the sole admissible source of fact.

The system prompt accordingly has two distinct functions. The first is to define the operational scope of the system: the role it occupies, the audience it addresses, the register it writes in, and the format its output takes. The second is to install hard constraints on that behaviour, of which the grounding constraint is the most consequential. A grounding constraint restricts the model to the retrieved context and specifies what to do when that context is inadequate, which converts an unanswerable query from a case the model improvises around into a case with a defined response. The two functions differ in kind: scope shapes how an answer is written, whereas a hard constraint determines whether an answer may be given at all. Listing~\ref{lst:prompt} illustrates both in a minimal example.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={A minimal system prompt combining an operational scope with two hard constraints.},label={lst:prompt}]
You are a customer service assistant for a parcel delivery company.

GROUNDING RULE
Answer only from the context provided below. Do not use information
from your training data, even where you are confident that it is
correct.

INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE
If the context does not contain the answer, reply with exactly:
The provided documents do not contain this information.

FORMAT
Answer in at most three sentences. Cite the identifier of every
document you use, in square brackets.
\end{lstlisting}

The first line fixes the operational scope. The two blocks that follow are hard constraints: one restricts the admissible source of fact, the other defines the response for the case in which no admissible source exists. The final block constrains form rather than content, and belongs to the first function. Each of these instructions is stated in a way that can be checked against an output, which is the property Chapter 4 requires of the requirement set $\Sigma$.

Whether an instruction of this kind is in fact obeyed is a separate question from whether it has been written. Because the system prompt is expressed in natural language rather than as a specification the generator is bound to satisfy, its effect faces the same instruction-following and prompt-sensitivity issues as any other natural-language instruction to a language model, discussed further in Chapter 3. A grounding constraint that is followed for one phrasing and disregarded for another provides weaker protection than its wording suggests. This is why the system prompt, formalized in Chapter 4 as a set of requirements together with a wording, is the object of experimental control in this thesis.

\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation and Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:finetuning}

Fine-tuning is the other established way of adapting a general-purpose language model to a specific application, and it is useful to state how it differs from retrieval before the use cases in Section~\ref{sec:usecase} are discussed. Fine-tuning continues training on domain-specific data and changes the model parameters $\theta$, so what is learned is absorbed into the parametric memory described in Section~\ref{sec:generator}. RAG leaves $\theta$ untouched and changes what is placed in the context window at inference time. The two are therefore not competing answers to one question. They act on different parts of the system, and the practical decision is which part a given requirement belongs to: the knowledge the system reasons over, or the way the system reasons and writes \citep{ovadia2024finetuning}.

For knowledge that is new, changing, or proprietary, retrieval is the better fit, for three reasons. The first is cost. Fine-tuning requires a training run over the model's parameters whenever the underlying information changes, whereas adding a document to a knowledge base requires only that the new chunks be embedded and indexed, which is a fraction of the computation and can be done continuously rather than in scheduled retraining cycles \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag}. The second is data freshness and revocability. A document can be added, corrected, or deleted and takes effect at the next query, while a fact absorbed into $\theta$ cannot be selectively removed without retraining; \citet{wang2024knowledgemechanisms} survey the difficulty of locating and editing individual facts inside model parameters. This matters wherever information is corrected, retracted, or must be deleted on request, and it is one reason retrieval is preferred in regulated settings, where sensitive data can remain in a controlled external store instead of being absorbed into the parameters \citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. The third is auditability. Because a RAG answer is produced from a specific set of retrieved chunks, each claim in the output can in principle be checked against the passage that supports it, and unsupported claims can be identified at the level of the individual statement \citep{niu2024ragtruth}. A fine-tuned model offers no comparable trace: the information is distributed across its parameters, and the model cannot indicate which training example a given assertion came from. Retrieval does not eliminate unsupported claims, as Section~\ref{sec:usecase} discusses, but it does make them detectable.

Fine-tuning remains the correct choice when the requirement concerns behaviour rather than facts \citep{zhou2023lima}. Three cases are typical. The first is specialised vocabulary: in a domain whose terminology, abbreviations, or notation are poorly represented in the training corpus, a model may misread the query or the retrieved evidence itself, and no amount of retrieval repairs a failure that occurs during interpretation. The second is strict output structure. A system that must emit a fixed JSON schema, a proprietary markup or a domain-specific programming language applies that constraint to every request, and a constraint that holds for every request is more reliably and more cheaply placed in the parameters than restated in every prompt. The third is register and tone, where a consistent style is wanted across many interactions rather than described anew each time. In each case what is being adjusted is how the model responds, not what it knows.

Framed this way, the two techniques are complementary rather than exclusive, and production systems often combine them: a model fine-tuned for the vocabulary and output format of its domain, retrieving the facts it reasons over from an external knowledge base. The separation matters for this thesis because behaviour can be controlled through either channel. Fine-tuning writes the desired behaviour into $\theta$; the system prompt requests it in natural language at inference time. The second channel is far cheaper and can be changed at any moment, which is why it is the one most systems rely on, but it is also the one whose effect is hardest to predict, since a natural-language instruction is not a specification with guaranteed semantics. This thesis holds $\theta$ fixed and studies that second channel.

\section{Use-Case of RAG}
\label{sec:usecase}

RAG systems are typically used where the needed knowledge is too large, too specific, or changes too often to be captured reliably by a model's parametric knowledge alone. Concrete deployments illustrate this across several domains: question-answering over enterprise and internal document collections \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag}, customer support systems that must answer from current product or policy documentation, coding assistants that retrieve from a specific codebase instead of relying on general programming knowledge, legal question-answering over case law and contract repositories \citep{pipitone2024legalbenchrag}, and clinical decision support that grounds its answers in current medical literature and institutional protocols \citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. In each of these cases, RAG is usually preferred over two alternatives. The first is fine-tuning the generator on the target document collection, which Section~\ref{sec:finetuning} discusses: the collections above change continuously, and retraining on each change is neither economical nor traceable. The second is extending the model's context window to include the whole document collection, which avoids retraining but does not scale to large collections and does not, by itself, make sure the model actually attends to the passages that are relevant to the query.

Despite these advantages, RAG does not remove the failure modes of purely parametric generation; it shifts them, and in some cases only partly fixes them. \citet{chen2024rgb} show that current language models tolerate a moderate amount of irrelevant retrieved content reasonably well, but still struggle to reject a query when none of the retrieved evidence is enough to answer it, to combine evidence spread across multiple retrieved chunks, and to stay robust when retrieved evidence is outdated or contradictory. \citet{niu2024ragtruth} further show that language models keep producing hallucinated content even when relevant evidence has been retrieved and given to them in context. This means that grounding a model in retrieved evidence does not, by itself, guarantee that its output stays faithful to that evidence. These failure modes are why the literature review in Chapter 3 and the experimental design in Chapters 4 and 5 build the query set to include directly answerable, multi-hop, conflicting, and insufficient-evidence cases, so the system prompt's effect on exactly these failure modes can be measured.

\section{Advanced RAG Architectures}
\label{sec:advanced}

The architecture described so far retrieves once per query and generates once from what was retrieved. Recent work relaxes both assumptions, and the resulting variants can be grouped by which stage of the pipeline they modify. The following summary is included for completeness; none of these variants is used in this thesis, which studies the standard architecture.

\begin{description}
    \item[Conversational RAG.] Retaining prior turns of a dialogue as state, so that a follow-up question can be resolved against what was already asked and answered. A query such as ``and the second one?'' is meaningless to a retriever on its own, so the conversation history is used either to rewrite it into a standalone query or, as \citet{liu2024chatqa} show, to train a retriever directly on multi-turn data, which reaches comparable accuracy at lower cost than rewriting.
    \item[Pre-retrieval optimisation.] Rewriting or expanding the query before it reaches the retriever. Hypothetical Document Embeddings (HyDE) generate a hypothetical answer to the query and embed that instead of the query itself, on the argument that an answer lies closer in embedding space to the passage containing it than a question does \citep{gao2023hyde}.
    \item[Routing and adaptive retrieval.] Deciding where and how much to retrieve. Where several collections or indexes are available, a routing step can select among them according to what the query is about, rather than searching one combined collection for everything. Adaptive-RAG goes further and varies the depth of retrieval with the difficulty of the query, answering simple questions directly and reserving multi-step retrieval for those that need it \citep{jeong2024adaptiverag}.
    \item[Self-correction and reflection.] Evaluating retrieved evidence before or during generation. Corrective RAG (CRAG) grades each retrieved document, decomposes the ones it keeps into smaller knowledge strips and discards the strips that are not relevant, falling back to a wider search when retrieval fails \citep{yan2024crag}. Self-RAG instead trains the model to decide for itself when to retrieve and to critique its own draft output, so that retrieval and generation interleave rather than run once each \citep{asai2024selfrag}.
    \item[Agentic RAG.] Treating retrieval as one action among several available to an agent. A meta-agent delegates to document-specific or tool-specific agents and combines their results, which turns a fixed pipeline into a workflow whose steps are chosen at run time \citep{singh2025agentic}.
\end{description}

Other work extends the architecture in directions that are orthogonal to this routing taxonomy: knowledge graphs structure the retrieved evidence rather than treating it as a flat set of passages \citep{edge2024graphrag}, and multimodal retrieval covers images, audio and video alongside text \citep{abootorabi2025multimodalrag}. A recent systematic review places these directions within a single trajectory away from single-shot, text-only retrieval \citep{oche2025systematicreview}. Understanding the standard architecture formalized in this thesis remains a prerequisite for all of them: each varies the retrieval function $R$, the generation function $G$, or the number of times they are applied, but none changes the underlying distribution $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ on which they still depend.

This thesis implements the standard architecture and none of the variants above. The reason is methodological. The research objective is to estimate the causal effect of the system prompt on the output, which Chapter 4 formalizes as the intervention $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ and which is identifiable only as long as the system prompt reaches the answer through a single path. Each of the variants breaks that condition. Under Self-RAG the model decides for itself when to retrieve \citep{asai2024selfrag}, so the system prompt influences which documents are fetched as well as how they are used. Under routing, corrective and agentic designs the prompt shapes the decisions of the router, the retrieval evaluator or the meta-agent, so the retrieved context $C$ ceases to be determined by the query and the corpus alone. Retrieval would then have to be written $p_\eta(z \mid q,D,S)$ rather than $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$, the arrow from $S$ into the retriever that Figure~\ref{fig:causal} deliberately omits would be present, and a measured difference between two prompt conditions could be attributed either to the prompt's effect on generation or to its effect on what was retrieved, with no way to separate the two. The routing policy would operate as a confounding variable, and the isolation of effects on which the experimental design rests would no longer hold.

The standard architecture is therefore the controlled baseline rather than a simplification adopted for convenience. It is the configuration in which the system prompt enters the pipeline at exactly one point, which is the property that makes the intervention identifiable. The direction of inference matters as well: because the variants above add routing, reflection or delegation around the retrieval and generation functions instead of replacing them, an effect established at this level bounds what can be expected once those mechanisms are introduced, whereas an effect measured inside an agentic pipeline could not be decomposed back into a prompt component and a routing component. It is also the configuration most widely deployed in practice, as Section~\ref{sec:usecase} describes, so the result is not restricted to a hypothetical case. Establishing the causal mechanics of prompt interventions at this level is a prerequisite for studying them in any richer pipeline, and extending the design to one of the variants above is left to future work.

\chapter{Literature Review}

Chapter 2 introduced the components of a RAG system. This chapter reviews three areas of literature relevant to studying how the system prompt affects such a system: Section 3.1 covers prompt sensitivity and instruction-following in language models in general, Section 3.2 covers retrieval-augmented generation specifically, its architecture, evaluation, and known failure modes, and Section 3.3 covers adversarial and competing instructions delivered through retrieved content. Section 3.4 brings all three together into the specific research gap this thesis addresses.

\section{Prompt Sensitivity and Instruction-Following in Language Models}

The first branch of literature covers prompt sensitivity and instruction-following in language models in general, without any retrieval component. These studies show that system prompts are a measurable and controllable way of shaping model behavior, and that this mechanism is sensitive to change at several levels, from prompt specificity down to plain rewording.

\citet{cheng2026systemprompts} study how different system prompts affect instruction-tuned language models for code generation, varying prompt specificity, prompting strategy, model, programming language, and decoding settings; their work directly motivates the controlled manipulation of system prompts in this thesis, while the present experiment extends the analysis to a RAG setting and focuses explicitly on system behaviour and system outcomes. \citet{mu2025robustness} investigate the robustness of system prompts as a mechanism for controlling LLM behaviour, particularly when system instructions interact with conflicting or adversarial user inputs, supporting the modelling of the system prompt as a behavioural control variable and motivating measuring whether a system actually follows the intended instructions. \citet{lu2024sensitivity} study prompt sensitivity across different models and tasks, examining how changes in prompt formulation affect model behaviour and output, which is relevant to the present research because it motivates distinguishing changes in the intended instruction from changes caused by the linguistic formulation of that instruction. \citet{zhuo2024prosa} propose methods for measuring and understanding prompt sensitivity across LLMs and tasks, investigating why seemingly small prompt changes can produce different model behaviour, and their work provides methodological support for treating prompt effects as measurable experimental phenomena rather than anecdotal observations.

\citet{salinas2024butterfly} investigate the sensitivity of LLM decisions to small perturbations in prompts, demonstrating that minor changes in prompt construction can sometimes produce substantial changes in model outputs, which motivates the use of controlled, minimal prompt interventions in this thesis. \citet{zheng2024helpful} investigate the effects of assigning different personas or roles through system prompts and examine whether these changes improve model performance; their findings are relevant to the distinction between changes in intended system behaviour and actual improvements in objective system performance. \citet{ying2024intuitive} examine how LLMs respond to conflicting prompts and information, including situations relevant to retrieval-augmented generation, and their work is particularly relevant to the thesis' interest in how system instructions influence a system when retrieved evidence is incomplete, conflicting, or inconsistent with other information. \citet{ruiz2026promptcarefully} experimentally investigate how LLMs respond to explicit behavioural rules provided through prompts and how these rules interact with changing task contingencies, supporting the proposed view of system prompts as specifications of behavioural policies rather than only textual context.

\citet{hua2025flaw} examine prompt sensitivity and show that some apparent differences between prompts can arise from the way model outputs are evaluated rather than from meaningful behavioural differences, which is important for the present research because it motivates using evaluation metrics that distinguish genuine changes in RAG behaviour and outcomes from superficial output variation. \citet{qin2026interactions} investigate prompt sensitivity through interactions between prompts and model inputs, moving beyond simple comparisons of final outputs to analyze how prompt changes affect model behaviour, and their approach is closely related to the present research's goal of developing a more structured account of how system prompts influence an AI system. \citet{sclar2024quantifying} show that language models are highly sensitive to meaning-preserving prompt formatting changes (e.g., delimiters, casing, list vs.\ prose), with performance swings of up to 76 accuracy points on some models despite no change in propositional content, which directly motivates isolating the Format condition ($S_{\theta_3}$) as a distinct linguistic axis in this thesis's experimental design, separate from lexical or syntactic variation.

Together, these studies make two points that shape the experimental design of this thesis. First, system prompts are not incidental configuration details but a measurable behavioral control variable: their effect on a model's output can be isolated and measured given a properly controlled experimental setup \citep{mu2025robustness,ruiz2026promptcarefully}. Second, prompt effects are confounded by several sources of variation at once: the wording of an instruction as distinct from its meaning \citep{lu2024sensitivity,zhuo2024prosa,salinas2024butterfly}, purely formatting-level changes that alter no actual content \citep{sclar2024quantifying}, and the evaluation method used to detect a behavioral difference in the first place \citep{hua2025flaw}. None of these studies, however, look at system prompts inside a retrieval-augmented setting, where system behavior also depends on retrieved evidence that can itself be noisy, conflicting, or insufficient; \citet{ying2024intuitive} is a partial exception, discussed further below.

\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation: Architecture, Evaluation, and Failure Modes}

The second branch of literature covers retrieval-augmented generation specifically: its architecture, its evaluation, and the failure modes it shows independent of any system-prompt manipulation.

\citet{lewis2020rag} introduce Retrieval-Augmented Generation, combining a pretrained neural retriever with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, and show that conditioning generation on retrieved evidence outperforms purely parametric models on knowledge-intensive tasks; their probabilistic formulation, in which retrieval is a distribution over documents and the retrieved document is marginalized out, is the direct basis for the model formalized in Chapter 4 of this thesis. \citet{gao2023survey} survey the development of RAG systems through \textbf{Naive}, \textbf{Advanced}, and \textbf{Modular} paradigms, organizing the field around a tripartite retrieval-generation-augmentation structure; their taxonomy supports treating retrieval and generation as separable functions whose interaction with the system prompt can be studied independently, as this thesis does. Two later developments extend this taxonomy in directions this thesis deliberately does not follow. \citet{edge2024graphrag} restructure the retrieval side into a knowledge graph, grouped into thematic communities and pre-summarized by an LLM, so that a query is answered by traversing entities and community summaries rather than by a flat nearest-neighbor lookup over $D$. \citet{singh2025agentic} and \citet{jin2025searchr1} restructure the generation side into an agent loop, in which the model itself decides when and what to retrieve, calling $R$ repeatedly and adaptively rather than once per query; \citet{jin2025searchr1} train this behaviour directly with reinforcement learning and report large gains over standard RAG baselines. Both directions relax exactly the components Chapter 4 holds fixed, $R$ in the first case and the single-call structure of $G$ in the second. For that reason this thesis retains \citeauthor{gao2023survey}'s simplest, Naive-RAG form: any change in the outcomes measured here can then be attributed to the system prompt, rather than to the added architectural complexity of a graph-structured retriever or an agentic generation loop.

\citet{es2023ragas} propose Ragas, a reference-free evaluation framework for RAG pipelines built around three metrics, \textbf{faithfulness}, \textbf{answer relevance}, and \textbf{context relevance}, that require no ground-truth human annotation; their faithfulness metric is closely related to the evidence-faithfulness measure used in this thesis, and motivates evaluating the effect of the system prompt independently of retrieval quality. \citet{chen2024rgb} construct the Retrieval-Augmented Generation Benchmark (RGB) and show that current language models tolerate a moderate amount of retrieval noise but struggle with negative rejection, information integration across multiple retrieved passages, and robustness to counterfactual or contradictory evidence; their four testbeds correspond closely to the directly-answerable, multi-hop, conflicting, and insufficient-evidence query categories used in the experimental design of this thesis. \citet{niu2024ragtruth} introduce RAGTruth, a manually annotated corpus of word-level hallucinations produced by RAG systems, and benchmark hallucination-detection methods across multiple language models; their finding that hallucination persists even when relevant evidence has been retrieved motivates measuring faithfulness as an outcome distinct from retrieval quality, as this thesis does.

Together, these studies establish the architecture, evaluation vocabulary, and known failure modes of RAG systems specifically. They show that a RAG system's output quality depends on both retrieval quality and how faithfully the generator uses the retrieved evidence \citep{es2023ragas,niu2024ragtruth}, and that current systems already struggle with certain evidence conditions, such as conflicting or insufficient retrieved evidence, even without any prompt manipulation \citep{chen2024rgb}. None of the studies in this branch, however, treat the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable; it is either fixed across conditions or not specified at all.

\section{Adversarial and Competing Instructions in Retrieved Content}

A third, closely related line of work studies what happens when instructions arrive not from the system prompt but from the retrieved content itself, competing with the system prompt for control of the model's behavior. Where the first branch shows that a model's own system prompt can be perturbed and still change behavior \citep{mu2025robustness,salinas2024butterfly}, \citet{greshake2023notwhat} show that an entirely different source of instruction, text hidden inside a document the model is told to process, can override the system prompt altogether. This is the adversarial extreme of the same instruction-competition problem, except the competing instruction arrives through the retrieved context $C$ rather than through $S$, a source this thesis deliberately holds fixed rather than varies. \citet{zhang2025poisoning} extend this observation into a RAG-specific benchmark, testing 13 poisoning attack methods and 7 defenses across 5 base question-answering datasets and 10 expanded variants; their findings sit alongside the observations of \citet{chen2024rgb} and \citet{niu2024ragtruth} from the second branch that retrieved evidence can already be noisy, incomplete, or contradictory, and show that it can also be made deliberately adversarial.

\medskip
Both studies, however, treat instruction competition exclusively as a security problem: an adversary deliberately crafts malicious content, and the goal is to detect or defend against it. Neither examines benign, non-adversarial linguistic variation of the system prompt itself, of the kind the prompt-sensitivity literature reviewed above studies outside of RAG, and of the kind this thesis's five conditions ($S_{\theta_0}$ through $S_{\theta_4}$) are built to isolate inside it. This distinction matters: this thesis is not concerned with whether a RAG system can be attacked, but with whether ordinary, well-intentioned rewordings of its own instructions change its behavior at all.

\section{Synthesis and Research Gap}

The first branch shows that system prompts are a measurable, controllable way of shaping language model behavior, sensitive even to small changes in wording, but studies this mostly outside of any retrieval-augmented setting. The second branch establishes the architecture, evaluation vocabulary, and known failure modes of RAG systems, including known problems with evidence integration, rejection, and faithfulness, but does not treat the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable. The third branch shows that instructions competing with the system prompt is a real and actively studied phenomenon in RAG systems, but only as a deliberate security threat delivered through retrieved content, never as benign, controlled linguistic variation of the system prompt itself. The overlap between all three branches is still largely unexamined. \citet{ying2024intuitive} is a partial exception: it looks at how LLMs respond to conflicting prompts and information in retrieval-relevant settings, but without holding retrieval and the underlying language model fixed as an explicit experimental control.

This thesis addresses that gap directly. It treats the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable inside a RAG pipeline, varying only its wording rather than its intent, while holding the query, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and underlying language model fixed, so that any change in system behavior and outcomes can be traced to the system prompt's wording alone. Chapter 4 builds the formal model that makes this control precise, describing the RAG system as a retrieval function combined with a generation function, with the system prompt as an explicit parameter of the generation function.

\chapter{Formal Model of a RAG System}

Chapter 3 identified a gap: prompt sensitivity is studied outside retrieval, and RAG evaluation does not treat the system prompt as a controlled variable. Closing it requires a model of the system precise enough to isolate the system prompt. This chapter builds that model on the probabilistic formulation of retrieval-augmented generation given by \citet{lewis2020rag}, and extends it by making the system prompt an explicit variable of the generation process.

\section{Modelling Objective}

The formalization serves two purposes. It states the probabilistic relationship between the user query, the retrieved documents and the generated output, and it provides the basis for treating the system prompt as an experimental intervention whose effect can be measured while the query, the knowledge base, the retriever and the language model are held constant.

The model can be read as a chain,
\[
(q,D) \;\longrightarrow\; p_\eta(z \mid q,D) \;\longrightarrow\; p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S) \;\longrightarrow\; p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S),
\]
in which the system prompt $S$ enters at the generation stage only. The research question of this thesis is then a question about that final distribution: what changes in $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ when $S$ is changed?

\section{Notation}

Table~\ref{tab:notation} lists the symbols used throughout. \citet{lewis2020rag} write the input sequence as $x$; this thesis writes it as $q$, since the input is always a user query, and otherwise keeps their notation.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Notation of the formal model.}
\label{tab:notation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Symbol & Meaning \\
\midrule
$q$                      & user query \\
$D = \{d_1,\ldots,d_N\}$ & knowledge base \\
$z \in D$                & a single retrieved document \\
$\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$     & the $K$ documents with the highest retrieval probability \\
$C$                      & retrieved context, the collection of documents given to the generator \\
$y = (y_1,\ldots,y_T)$   & generated output sequence of $T$ tokens \\
$S \in \mathcal{S}$      & system prompt \\
$\eta$                   & parameters of the retriever \\
$\theta$                 & parameters of the generator \\
$Y$                      & the output viewed as a random variable \\
$M(Y)$                   & a measured property of the output \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Probabilistic Retrieval}

Retrieval is represented as a probability distribution over documents rather than as a single choice:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:retrieval}
p_\eta(z \mid q,D).
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval} reads as the probability the retriever assigns to document $z$, given the query $q$ and the knowledge base $D$. Writing retrieval this way means the retriever never has to commit to one document being the correct one. It spreads probability over the candidates, and documents it considers more relevant receive more of it.

A knowledge base may contain a very large number of documents, and evaluating all of them is not practical. The formulation therefore keeps only the $K$ documents with the highest retrieval probability,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:topk}
\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D) = \operatorname*{TopK}_{z \in D} \; p_\eta(z \mid q,D),
\end{equation}

and the retrieved context handed to the generator is the resulting collection,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:context}
C = \{z_1,\ldots,z_K\}, \qquad z_k \in \mathcal{Z}_K(q,D).
\end{equation}

The distinction between $z$ and $C$ matters later. The symbol $z$ denotes one candidate document inside the probabilistic formulation, over which the model will sum; $C$ denotes the concrete set of documents the running system retrieves and logs.

\section{Prompt-Conditioned Generation}

\citet{lewis2020rag} write the generator at the level of a single token as $p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1})$: the probability of the next token given the query, one retrieved document, and the tokens already produced. This thesis adds the system prompt as a further conditioning variable:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:token}
p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:token} is the point at which this thesis departs from the original formulation, and the departure is deliberately small. The generator is the same model with the same parameters $\theta$; what changes is that the system prompt is now written into the conditioning information rather than left implicit.

Because generation is autoregressive, the probability of a complete output sequence follows from the token probabilities by multiplication:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sequence}
p_\theta(y \mid q,z,S) = \prod_{i=1}^{T} p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

\section{Marginalizing over Retrieved Documents}
\label{sec:ragdist}

Equation~\eqref{eq:sequence} gives the probability of an output under one particular document $z$. Since the system does not know which retrieved document is the one that supports the answer, the model averages over the candidates, weighting each by its retrieval probability. \citet{lewis2020rag} give two ways of doing this, which differ only in where the sum is placed.

\subsection{RAG-Sequence}

RAG-Sequence assumes that a single retrieved document is responsible for the whole answer. The probability of an output is the weighted average, over documents, of the probability that document would produce that entire sequence:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragseq}
p_{\text{RAG-Sequence}}(y \mid q,D,S) \;\approx\; \sum_{z \in \mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)} p_\eta(z \mid q,D) \prod_{i=1}^{T} p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

The sum stands outside the product, which is what encodes the assumption: one document is fixed, the entire sequence is generated from it, and only then is the next document considered.

\subsection{RAG-Token}

RAG-Token makes the weaker assumption that different parts of an answer may come from different documents. The average over documents is taken separately at every token:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragtok}
p_{\text{RAG-Token}}(y \mid q,D,S) \;\approx\; \prod_{i=1}^{T} \sum_{z \in \mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)} p_\eta(z \mid q,D) \, p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

The whole difference between the two is the order of the two operators: $\sum_z \prod_i$ against $\prod_i \sum_z$. RAG-Sequence commits to one document for the length of the answer; RAG-Token lets one document supply the first half of a sentence and another the second, which is the more flexible assumption and the one better suited to answers that combine several sources.

Both are written here as $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ where the distinction does not matter, and it does not matter for the experiment: the system prompt $S$ appears in exactly the same place in both, inside the generator and nowhere else.

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragdist}
p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S).
\end{equation}

\section{The System Prompt: Requirements and Wording}

The system prompt enters Equation~\eqref{eq:token} as a single symbol, but it is a text with internal structure, and the experiment varies that structure deliberately. A prompt states a set of requirements and states them in particular words. Writing the two separately gives

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prompt}
S = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi), \qquad \Sigma = \{\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m\},
\end{equation}

where each requirement $\sigma_k$ is a property an answer either has or does not have, so that $\sigma_k(y) \in \{0,1\}$ can be checked on any output, and $\varphi$ is the wording in which the requirements are expressed. Two prompts built from the same $\Sigma$ ask for the same thing; two built from the same $\varphi$ say things the same way.

Five prompt conditions are used. The first four hold $\Sigma$ fixed and change only the wording; the fifth changes the requirements themselves.

\begin{description}
    \item[$S_0 = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi_0)$ --- Canonical.] Plain imperative prose. The reference form.
    \item[$S_1 = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi_1)$ --- Lexical.] Synonym substitution, register held fixed.
    \item[$S_2 = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi_2)$ --- Syntactic.] Clause reordering, active $\leftrightarrow$ passive, imperative $\leftrightarrow$ declarative.
    \item[$S_3 = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi_3)$ --- Format.] Prose $\rightarrow$ bulleted list, punctuation, casing, delimiters.
    \item[$S_4 = \rho(\Sigma',\varphi_0)$ --- Deontic.] Modal force: must / should / please try to.
\end{description}

If the system responded only to what a prompt requires and not to how it is written, $S_0$ through $S_3$ would produce the same output distribution, since they differ in $\varphi$ alone. Whether they do is the central empirical question. The format condition follows \citet{sclar2024quantifying}, who report large performance differences from formatting changes of this kind. The fifth condition is written with a different requirement set because modal force is part of what is being demanded rather than part of its phrasing, and it is kept separate in the analysis for that reason.

\section{The System Prompt as an Intervention}
\label{sec:intervention}

The prompt appears in the generator, in Equation~\eqref{eq:token}, and not in the retriever, in Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval}. The model is therefore written with

\[
p_\eta(z \mid q,D) \qquad \text{rather than} \qquad p_\eta(z \mid q,D,S),
\]

which states that the system prompt is supplied to the generator but plays no part in choosing documents. In the notation of \citet{pearl2009causality}, $S$ has an effect on $y$ and no effect on $z$. Figure~\ref{fig:causal} shows the resulting structure.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[
