\caption{The system prompt $S$ and the output $y$ are drawn in bold. $S$ enters the generator and has no arrow into the retriever, which is what allows a change in $y$ to be attributed to a change in $S$.}
\label{fig:causal}
\end{figure}

For the effect of the prompt to be interpretable, the following are held constant across conditions. The query is fixed at $q = q_0$ and the knowledge base at $D = D_0$. The retriever and its parameters are fixed at $\eta = \eta_0$, and the language model at $\theta = \theta_0$, so the model is neither retrained nor replaced between conditions. Retrieval is assumed prompt-independent,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:noedge}
p_\eta(z \mid q,D,S) = p_\eta(z \mid q,D),
\end{equation}

which holds by construction for the single-shot architecture of Chapter 2 and is enforced in the experiment by recording the retrieved context once and reusing it. Generation, by contrast, is prompt-dependent: although $\theta$ stays fixed, the conditional distribution it defines may differ between $S_0$ and $S_j$. Finally, decoding is stochastic, so the same configuration can produce different outputs on different runs. Writing this randomness as $\varepsilon$,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:noise}
Y = G(q,z,S;\theta,\varepsilon),
\end{equation}

which is why each condition is repeated several times in Chapter 5.

\section{The Causal Effect}

Setting the system prompt to a fixed value is an intervention, written $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ following \citet{pearl2009causality}. The notation separates \textit{setting} a variable from merely \textit{observing} it: observing that two systems with different prompts give different answers leaves open that something else differed too, whereas setting the prompt while holding the rest fixed does not. The output under condition $S_j$ is the random variable

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:potential}
Y^{(j)} \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S_j).
\end{equation}

Let $M(Y)$ denote a measured property of the output. The causal effect of prompt condition $S_j$ relative to the baseline $S_0$ is the difference in the expected value of that property:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:estimand}
\boxed{\;
\tau_j \;=\; \mathbb{E}\bigl[M(Y) \mid \mathrm{do}(S = S_j)\bigr] \;-\; \mathbb{E}\bigl[M(Y) \mid \mathrm{do}(S = S_0)\bigr].
\;}
\end{equation}

Written out through the RAG distribution, this is

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:estimand-explicit}
\tau_j = \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_j)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr] - \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_0)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr],
\end{equation}

which connects the probability model of Section~\ref{sec:ragdist} directly to the quantity the experiment estimates. Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is what Chapter 5 measures.

Two points about the interpretation. The intervention is on the conditioning variable $S$, not on the model parameters: $\theta$ remains $\theta_0$ throughout, and the prompt changes what the generator is conditioned on rather than what it has learned. And because $S$ is assigned by the experimenter rather than observed, no further assumption about hidden common causes is needed; what remains to be established is only that the conditions in Section~\ref{sec:intervention} actually hold in the implementation.

Which property $M$ should be is left open here. Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} describes what that property has to capture, and Section~\ref{sec:metrics} fixes it.

\section{Grounding the Knowledge Base in Established Datasets}
\label{sec:knowledgebase}

The knowledge base has so far been written as a set $D = \{d_1,\ldots,d_N\}$ of unanalysed objects, and the model says nothing about where those objects come from. Filling $D$ is a methodological decision rather than a further step of formalization, and it is settled here because the effect defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is only as informative as the corpus and the queries it is estimated over.

Two routes are available. The first is to define the corpus theoretically: fix a set of facts about a small invented subject area, generate documents from those facts, and derive the query set from the resulting support structure. Its advantage is that every fact is known to the experimenter by construction. Its defect is that the data model and the hypothesis are then written by the same hand, so the difficulty of the queries, the density of distractors and the frequency of contradictions all become free parameters chosen in full knowledge of the effect they are meant to reveal. A result obtained on such a corpus is also incomparable with anything else in the field, because no other system has ever been run on it.

The second route, taken here, is to instantiate $D$ and the query set from established question-answer datasets. Three properties recommend it. The questions are naturally occurring rather than authored for the experiment, so the query distribution is not fitted to the hypothesis it is used to test. The corpora are public, versioned and split in a documented way, so a third party can repeat the measurement without reconstructing a private data generator. And because published results for a wide range of retrieval and generation systems already exist on these datasets, the effect estimated in Chapter 5 can be read against a background of prior work instead of standing alone. Reproducibility and comparability are properties of the benchmark, not of the formalism, and no amount of additional notation supplies them.

Four datasets are used, chosen so that each covers a failure mode the others do not.

\begin{description}
    \item[Natural Questions (NQ).] Aggregated from queries genuinely issued to Google Search, each paired with a Wikipedia article and an answer span marked by a human annotator \citep{kwiatkowski2019nq}. Because the questions were asked before any answer was sought, they carry the ambiguity and underspecification of real information needs rather than the clean phrasing of purpose-written test items. NQ is the primary open-domain benchmark of the canonical RAG literature: \citet{lewis2020rag} report their central results on it, and the open-domain question answering line of work that followed has treated it as the default point of comparison. It therefore supplies the setting in which a claim about grounding behaviour is legible to other researchers.

    \item[MS MARCO.] Built from queries sampled from Bing search logs, with candidate passages retrieved for each query and free-form answers written by human judges \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}. Its passage-ranking split contributes the relevance judgements on which dense retrievers are trained and evaluated, and it is the corpus on which dense passage retrieval was first shown to outperform sparse lexical baselines \citep{karpukhin2020dpr}. For the present purpose it governs the retrieval half of the pipeline: query-passage pairs whose relevance was judged externally rather than asserted by the experimenter, which is precisely what a study of the system prompt requires, since the prompt must be shown to act on the generator and not on the evidence reaching it.

    \item[TyDi QA.] A typologically diverse benchmark spanning eleven languages selected to differ in morphology, word order and script, with questions written by speakers who had not seen the answer and were therefore genuinely uncertain of it \citep{clark2020tydiqa}. It tests whether retrieval and generation survive conditions in which the query language differs from the language the generator was predominantly trained on. This matters for the question of this thesis because an instruction given in one language must still govern behaviour over evidence in another; a prompt effect that holds only in English is a weaker finding than one that holds across the languages TyDi QA covers.

    \item[Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU).] A multiple-choice benchmark of 57 subjects reaching from elementary mathematics to professional law and clinical medicine, designed to probe knowledge acquired during pretraining rather than skill at any single task \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}. Unlike the other three it ships no retrieval corpus, and that absence is what makes it useful: the closed-book score marks where the parametric knowledge of a model ends. The difference between that score and the score the same model reaches when relevant evidence is retrieved and supplied is a direct measure of what retrieval contributes, and it is the setting in which the failure to use supplied evidence, rather than the failure to possess knowledge, becomes visible.
\end{description}

The four are not interchangeable. NQ and MS MARCO establish the open-domain retrieval setting in which RAG is normally evaluated, TyDi QA supplies the multilingual stress case, and MMLU marks the boundary at which parametric memory gives out and retrieved evidence has to take over. Between them they provide the queries $q$, the documents that populate $D$, and, through their annotations, the reference answer $a_q$ against which an output can be judged. Write $\Phi$ for the set of facts those annotations hold true and $\Phi_d \subseteq \Phi$ for the facts stated by a document $d \in D$. The point of the empirical route is that $\Phi$ is fixed by the annotation, not by the experimenter.

What they do not provide is a definition of a good answer. An annotation records which answer is correct; it says nothing about whether an answer is grounded in the evidence actually retrieved, whether it acknowledges a contradiction between two retrieved documents, or whether it obeys the requirements the system prompt states. Those properties are the object of this thesis, and defining them is the subject of the next section.

\section{What Counts as a Good Answer}
\label{sec:goodanswer}

The preceding sections describe what the system does and how the influence of the system prompt can be separated from everything else that shapes an output. They say nothing about what would count as an improvement. Any comparison between prompt conditions presupposes a standard against which outputs are judged, and no such standard follows from the architecture itself. It has to be stated, and stating it belongs to the model rather than to the implementation: a measurement is only as trustworthy as the notion of quality it estimates, and that notion has to be fixed before any output is inspected.

Quality in a retrieval-augmented system is not one property but several, and they can fail independently of one another. The first is groundedness. An answer should rest on the documents the retriever actually supplied, and an answer that introduces material from nowhere has failed even if the material happens to be true. The second is relevance. An answer should address the question that was asked and address it directly, so that hedging, padding and drift towards a neighbouring question all count as failures, as does supplying unrelated material where the evidence will not support a reply at all. The third is correctness. An answer should agree with what is in fact the case, which is a different demand from the first and is treated separately here for a reason given below. The fourth is obedience. Whatever the system prompt asks of the output, in length, in form, in the handling of missing evidence, should be visible in the output itself, since a prompt that is not followed cannot be said to have any effect on quality at all.

Groundedness and correctness are kept apart deliberately, because collapsing them would hide the failure this thesis is concerned with. The retrieved context is neither complete nor guaranteed to be free of error. An answer can therefore be correct while ignoring the evidence entirely, drawing instead on what the generator already knows, and it can use the evidence faithfully and still be wrong because the evidence was wrong. A system that scores well on correctness alone tells us nothing about whether retrieval was used, which is precisely the behaviour a system prompt is supposed to govern.

Answers are rarely either flawless or worthless, so a standard that only sorts them into good and bad discards most of what an experiment can observe. What is needed instead is a graded notion of shortfall, one that records how far an answer falls from the standard and in which of the four respects it does so. The four are also not equally serious, and their relative weight is a property of the setting rather than of the model. A claim that no retrieved document supports is a grave failure in a clinical assistant and a minor irritation in a consumer search tool, and any honest account of quality has to leave room for that difference to be recorded rather than assumed away. Grading the shortfall in this way settles the question of abstention without a separate rule: where a wrong answer is treated as costlier than an admission of ignorance, declining to answer becomes the better option exactly when the evidence will not support a reply, which is the long-standing trade-off between committing errors and withholding judgement described by \citet{chow1970reject}.

The evaluation of this thesis therefore asks a single question. Holding the query, the knowledge base and the retrieval procedure fixed, does replacing one system prompt with another move the outputs of the system towards this standard or away from it, and does the direction of that movement survive when the retrieved evidence is degraded? A baseline prompt supplies the point of comparison, and every other condition is read against it. Because the four requirements are separable, the answer can be reported both as a single summary of overall quality and as a breakdown showing which of the four the prompt improved and which, if any, it made worse.

The formal definitions this evaluation rests on are introduced in Chapter 5. That chapter states the set of acceptable answers precisely, gives the cost function that grades an answer's shortfall against the four requirements, names the measure used to estimate each of the four, and defines the quantity that expresses the effect of a prompt condition relative to the baseline. It also specifies the corpus, the query set, the experimental conditions and the statistical procedure through which that quantity is estimated.

\chapter{Experimental Setup}

This chapter turns the model of Chapter 4 into a concrete procedure. Chapter 4 described the system as the distribution $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:ragdist} and the effect of a prompt as the intervention $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ on it. The system under test is the standard architecture, for the reasons of experimental control given in Section~\ref{sec:advanced}. The chapter describes how the corpus and the query set are assembled from the datasets of Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase}, which conditions the system is run under, how the parts of the cost $\ell$ are measured, and how the effect $\tau_j$ is estimated. All experiments are implemented in Python. Every metric used below is taken from published RAG evaluation work, and every design parameter follows a precedent named in the text. Section~\ref{sec:metrics} first states formally the standard that Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} set out in general terms, so that no metric is chosen before the notion of quality it estimates has been fixed.

\section{Goal and Design}
\label{sec:goal}

The experiment manipulates exactly one component of the pipeline. The system prompt is the sole independent variable; every other part of the system is held at a fixed value for the whole of the data collection. This is a deliberate control rather than a convenience. A retrieval-augmented system has many parts that are known to move answer quality on their own, and each of them, if left free, would offer an alternative explanation for any difference observed between prompt conditions. Fixing them removes those explanations in advance, so that a difference in the measured cost can be attributed to the prompt and to nothing else.

The components held constant are the following. The generator is a single instruction-tuned language model, fixed in both its identity and its parameters $\theta$ for the whole of the data collection, and its decoding temperature and maximum output length are fixed with it, since \citet{salinas2024butterfly} show that decoding settings move output quality on their own and would otherwise be confounded with the prompt. Queries and passages are encoded by one and the same embedding model, applied to both sides of the comparison in the manner of the dense retriever of \citet{karpukhin2020dpr}. The vector database and the index built over it, implemented with the similarity-search library of \citet{johnson2021faiss}, are constructed once before any condition is run and are never rebuilt thereafter. The chunking strategy $\chi$ places one passage in each chunk, following the retrieval-unit study of \citet{chen2024densex}, and the retrieval depth is fixed at $K = 5$ passages per query, the depth used in the distractor setting of \citet{yang2018hotpotqa}. Because the retriever and its parameters $\eta$ are determined entirely by these choices, they do not vary either. None of these is re-tuned, re-indexed or re-selected at any point during the experiment, and no condition is run against a rebuilt index.

Fixing the retrieval side has a second consequence that matters for the causal claim. Because $\eta$, $\chi$, $K$, the embedding model and the index are all constant, the set $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:topk} depends only on the query. For a given query the same passages are retrieved in every prompt condition, so the evidence reaching the generator is not merely comparable across conditions but identical. Any change in the answer therefore originates at the generator, which is exactly the localisation the intervention of Chapter 4 assumes.

One further factor varies, and it is not a second treatment. The quality of the evidence placed in the context is set to four levels, described in Section~\ref{sec:conditions}, because a prompt effect that appears only when the evidence is clean would be of little practical interest. Evidence quality is a controlled robustness factor: the contrast of interest is always between prompt conditions, estimated separately within each level, and the levels themselves are not compared in order to make a claim about them. Writing $c_{ik}$ for the $k$-th context given to query $q_i$, one observation is

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:design}
y_{ijkr} \;\sim\; p_{\mathrm{RAG}}\bigl(y \;\big|\; q_i,\ \mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik}),\ \mathrm{do}(S = S_j)\bigr), \qquad r = 1,\ldots,n,
\end{equation}

where $j \in \{0,\ldots,4\}$ is the prompt condition, $k \in \{1,\ldots,4\}$ the evidence level, and $r \in \{1,\ldots,n\}$ the repetition. The five prompt conditions and the four evidence levels are fully crossed, giving $20$ cells. Every query is run in every cell, so each query serves as its own control and no difference between conditions can be produced by a difference in the queries they were asked.

Within a cell the only remaining source of variation is the random term $\varepsilon$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:noise}, which is why each cell is repeated $n = 3$ times. \citet{salinas2024butterfly} show that repeated sampling is needed before a difference between prompts can be separated from decoding noise. Twenty cells, eighty queries and three repetitions give 4800 generations in the main experiment, to which the multilingual replication described in Section~\ref{sec:corpus} adds a further 600.

\section{Knowledge Base and Query Set}
\label{sec:corpus}

The corpus is collected, not generated. Following Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase}, the knowledge base $D$ is assembled from the retrieval collections of the datasets named there: Wikipedia passages carrying the annotated answers of Natural Questions \citep{kwiatkowski2019nq} and passages from the MS MARCO collection \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}. The index holds the gold passage or passages for every query in the query set together with a distractor pool sampled from the same two collections, $N = 800$ passages in total. Nothing in $D$ is written for this thesis, and the annotations, not the experimenter, fix which passage answers which query.

What the empirical route gives up is the closed world. On an invented corpus the fact set $\Phi$ is complete by construction, so any claim in an output can be checked against it. Here $\Phi$ contains the annotated gold answers and the facts stated by the passages in $D$, which is a subset of everything true. Correctness in the sense of Equation~\eqref{eq:ideal} is therefore evaluated only over the claims that bear on $a_q$: an output asserting some further true fact absent from $\Phi$ is not counted against requirement 2. Section~\ref{sec:limits} states what this costs. The gain is that the retrieval judgements, the answer annotations and the queries themselves come from outside the experiment, so the difficulty of the query set was not chosen by the person testing the hypothesis, and a third party can rebuild the index from the published datasets.

Each passage forms one chunk, and passages are used at the length at which they were published, which runs to between two and six sentences. \citet{chen2024densex} show that the size of the retrieval unit changes both retrieval quality and answer quality, so it is fixed here and never varied. Retrieval returns the $K = 5$ chunks with the highest retrieval probability, the set $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:topk}. The distractor pool is sized so that the top five contain on average one or two gold passages and three or four distractors, close to the two-gold-and-eight-distractor ratio of the distractor setting of \citet{yang2018hotpotqa}. The position of the gold passages inside the context is randomised and recorded, since \citet{liu2024lostmiddle} report that accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits.

The query set contains $N_Q = 80$ queries, sampled from the datasets so that the reference answer $a_q$ is given by annotation rather than by construction. The four categories follow the four testbeds of \citet{chen2024rgb} and hold twenty queries each, and they are balanced, because each one exposes a different way for an answer to fall short of Equation~\eqref{eq:ideal}: single-passage grounding, the integration of several passages, evidence that contradicts what the model already holds, and evidence that will not support any answer at all. Since the categories can no longer be read off a known support structure, each is obtained by a documented sampling rule:

\begin{description}
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}}$.] Twenty Natural Questions items whose annotation marks a single short answer span inside one Wikipedia passage. One passage suffices, so a failure here is a failure to use evidence that was present. This is the noise-robustness testbed of \citet{chen2024rgb}.
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}}$.] Twenty MS MARCO items whose human-written answer draws on two or more of the passages marked relevant for that query. The annotation identifies which passages those are, so the requirement to combine evidence is externally recorded rather than asserted, which makes this the information-integration testbed.
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{conflict}}$.] Twenty items with a single unambiguous annotated answer, drawn from both collections, for which a counterfactual variant of the gold passage is produced by entity substitution following \citet{longpre2021conflicts}. Gold and counterfactual passage are placed in the index together, so the retrieved context states a fact and its opposite. This is the counterfactual-robustness testbed, and it is the category on which evidence is set against whatever the model already holds.
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{insufficient}}$.] Twenty items for which $D$ contains no passage carrying the answer: Natural Questions items the annotators marked unanswerable from the retrieved page, and MMLU items \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} whose subject matter is not covered by the indexed passages. Both are cases where the correct behaviour is to decline, which makes this the negative-rejection testbed and the only category on which abstention can be observed.
\end{description}

Public datasets carry a risk an invented corpus does not: the generator may have seen the questions and their answers during pretraining. This is why the empty-context quantity $\pi_i$ of Section~\ref{sec:conditions} is a primary control here rather than a formality. Every query is first run under $c^{\varnothing}$ across all five prompt conditions, and $\pi_i$, the share of those runs answered correctly with no context at all, is recorded before the main analysis. Results are reported over the full query set and again over the subset with $\pi_i = 0$, which is the subset on which retrieval was actually tested. \citet{chen2024rgb} and \citet{longpre2021conflicts} report accuracy without documents for the same reason.

A separate subset covers the multilingual case. Forty TyDi QA queries \citep{clark2020tydiqa}, drawn from languages other than English and paired with their annotated passages, are run under $c^{\text{ret}}$ in all five prompt conditions, adding 600 generations. The system prompt is given in English throughout while the query and the evidence are not, which is the case a deployed assistant faces and the one in which an instruction has the furthest to travel. This subset is analysed separately and reported as a replication rather than pooled with the main design.

\section{Experimental Conditions}
\label{sec:conditions}

The five prompt conditions are those of Chapter 4. $S_0$ through $S_3$ are built from the same requirement set $\Sigma$ in four wordings; $S_4$ is built from a modified set $\Sigma'$ that changes the modal force. The requirement set $\Sigma$ used throughout asks the system to answer only from the retrieved documents, to say when the documents do not contain the answer, to report disagreement between documents, and to stay within a stated length. Each of these is a checkable property $\sigma_k(y) \in \{0,1\}$, in the sense of the verifiable instructions of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}.

The four evidence levels are all produced by setting $C$ directly rather than by changing the corpus and retrieving again, which is what keeps them compatible with the control described in Section~\ref{sec:goal}: the embedding model, the index, the chunking and the retrieval depth are never touched, and no level is obtained by re-running a modified retriever. Setting $C$ also isolates the effect on generation, since the retrieved passages enter the generator directly in Equation~\eqref{eq:token}, whereas changing $D$ would measure the corruption and the retriever's reaction to it together. Each level is an intervention $\mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik})$ in the sense of Chapter 4.

The first level, written $c^{\text{gold}}$, supplies only the passages the annotation marks as supporting the answer, and so records how the system behaves when the evidence is as good as it can be. The second, $c^{\text{ret}}$, supplies the genuine top-$K$ output of the fixed retriever, whatever that happens to contain on a given query; it is the deployment condition, and the only level at which the system is observed as it would actually run. The third, $c^{\text{corr}}$, takes the gold passages and negates the facts that support the answer, following the entity-substitution method of \citet{longpre2021conflicts} and matching the counterfactual documents of \citet{chen2024rgb}; it sets the retrieved evidence directly against whatever the generator holds in its parameters. The fourth, $c^{\varnothing}$, supplies no context whatever and therefore isolates parametric knowledge on its own. None of the four is compared against another in order to make a claim about evidence quality; each exists so that the prompt contrast can be estimated again under different conditions.

The empty level $c^{\varnothing}$ is the control for what the model already knows. Figure~\ref{fig:causal} shows $\theta$ as a cause of the answer, and without this condition its contribution cannot be separated from that of the retrieved evidence. Let $\pi_i$ denote how often query $q_i$ is answered correctly with no context at all. A query with high $\pi_i$ never tested retrieval. \citet{chen2024rgb} report accuracy without documents alongside accuracy with counterfactual documents for the same reason, and \citet{longpre2021conflicts} show that models often answer from memory when the context contradicts it. Results are therefore reported over the full query set and again restricted to queries with $\pi_i = 0$. Because the queries are drawn from public datasets that the generator may have seen during pretraining, a high $\pi_i$ is expected for part of the query set rather than being the exception, which is why the restricted analysis carries more weight here than it would on an invented corpus.

\section{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} described in general terms what an answer has to do to count as a good one. This section states that standard, names the measures used to estimate each part of it, and fixes the scope of the measurement.

Every metric used here is a generation-stage metric, and retrieval-stage measures are excluded by design. The reason is structural rather than practical. The intervention of Equation~\eqref{eq:potential} sets the system prompt $S$, and $S$ enters the model at one place only: the generator term $p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:token}. The retrieval term $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval} contains no $S$, so retrieval is upstream of the intervention and cannot respond to it. Section~\ref{sec:goal} fixes the embedding model, the index, the chunking and the retrieval depth in addition, so the retrieved set $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ is not merely unaffected in expectation but literally the same set of passages in all five prompt conditions for a given query. Measures of ranking quality such as mean reciprocal rank or context precision are therefore constant across conditions by construction; a difference in them would be an arithmetic impossibility rather than a finding, and would indicate a fault in the implementation. Retrieval quality is established once, as a property of the fixed pipeline, and thereafter belongs to the experimental control rather than to the results. What the experiment measures is confined to what lies downstream of the intervention: given the same evidence in every condition, what the generator does with it.

The standard itself is stated first. An answer is a piece of text, and many texts can say the same thing, so what is defined is a set of acceptable answers rather than one ideal string. Write $\Phi_y$ for the facts an answer asserts and $\Phi_C$ for the facts contained in the retrieved context. An answer is good when it meets four requirements:

\begin{description}
    \item[Grounded.] It asserts only facts that are in the retrieved context: $\Phi_y \subseteq \Phi_C$.
    \item[Relevant.] It addresses the question that was asked, and where the evidence will not support an answer it says so rather than producing something off-target.
    \item[Correct.] It agrees with the reference answer supplied by the dataset annotation.
    \item[Obedient.] It meets every requirement the system prompt states: $\sigma_k(y) = 1$ for all $\sigma_k \in \Sigma$.
\end{description}

The set of answers meeting all four is written

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ideal}
\mathcal{Y}^{*}(q,C) = \bigl\{\, y \;:\; y \text{ meets requirements 1 to 4} \,\bigr\}.
\end{equation}

Let $n_k(y)$ count how often answer $y$ breaks requirement $k$, and let $\lambda_k$ record how serious that breach is in the setting the system is built for. The cost of an answer is

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:loss}
\ell(y) \;=\; \sum_{k=1}^{4} \lambda_k \, n_k(y),
\end{equation}

so that $\ell(y) = 0$ exactly when $y \in \mathcal{Y}^{*}(q,C)$ and larger values mean worse answers. Taking $M = \ell$ as the measured property in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} gives the main quantity of this thesis:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:risk}
\boxed{\;
\tau_j \;=\; \mathbb{E}\bigl[\ell(Y) \mid \mathrm{do}(S = S_j)\bigr] - \mathbb{E}\bigl[\ell(Y) \mid \mathrm{do}(S = S_0)\bigr].
\;}
\end{equation}

A negative $\tau_j$ means the prompt condition produces better answers than the baseline.

The first three requirements are estimated by the three generation-stage measures that the survey of \citet{TODO_RAG_Eval_Survey} identifies as the standard evaluation targets for the generation half of a retrieval-augmented system. They are adopted here rather than defined anew, and each is suited to this particular setup for a reason worth stating.

\begin{description}
    \item[Faithfulness, also called groundedness.] Whether the answer is derived exclusively from the retrieved context, so that every claim it makes can be traced to a supplied passage and nothing is introduced from elsewhere. This is the measure the experiment turns on. Four of the five prompt conditions state a grounding constraint, and faithfulness is what reveals whether that constraint is obeyed in practice or merely present in the wording. Because the retrieved evidence is identical across conditions, a change in faithfulness cannot be attributed to a change in what was available to the generator, and hallucination becomes observable as a property of the prompt rather than of the retrieval. Faithfulness estimates the first term $n_1$ of the cost, and is scored in the reference-free manner of \citet{es2023ragas}.

    \item[Answer relevance.] Whether the answer addresses the question that was actually asked, as opposed to a neighbouring one, and whether it does so directly. A system prompt can degrade an answer without making it false. Instructions that stress caution can produce hedging and evasion, instructions that stress completeness can produce padding, and instructions that impose a format can pull the response away from the question. Relevance captures these failures, which faithfulness and correctness both miss, and it is also where abstention is read: on the queries whose evidence is insufficient, an answer that declines and says why is responsive, whereas one that supplies unrelated material is not. Answer relevance estimates the second term $n_2$, and is likewise among the reference-free measures of \citet{es2023ragas}.

    \item[Correctness.] Whether the answer agrees with the ground-truth answer recorded in the dataset annotation. This is the end-to-end measure of whether the system was of any use, and it is available here precisely because the query set of Section~\ref{sec:corpus} is drawn from annotated question-answer datasets rather than constructed for the experiment. Correctness and faithfulness come apart under the corrupted evidence level $c^{\text{corr}}$: an answer that repeats a corrupted passage is faithful but wrong, and one that overrides it from memory is right but ungrounded. Reporting both is what makes that distinction visible. Correctness estimates the third term $n_3$, and is evaluated as accuracy against the annotation in the sense of \citet{chen2024rgb}.
\end{description}

The fourth requirement is measured separately, because it concerns the prompt rather than the evidence. Instruction adherence is scored as prompt-level strict accuracy in the sense of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}: each requirement in $\Sigma$ is a checkable property of the output, a length limit is checked by counting tokens and a citation requirement by parsing document identifiers, and an answer counts as adherent only if it satisfies every requirement at once. This measure is retained alongside the three above because the question of this thesis is whether a system prompt is followed, and the three generation measures record the consequences of following it without recording the following itself. Adherence estimates the fourth and last term $n_4$. $\Sigma$ is the same for $S_0$ through $S_3$, so adherence is directly comparable across those four conditions; $S_4$ uses $\Sigma'$ and is reported separately.

These four measures are described here at a conceptual level, and deliberately so: what matters for the argument is which failure each one detects and why that failure is informative about the system prompt, not the particular arithmetic by which a score is produced. Readers who want the underlying formulations, including the decomposition of an answer into atomic claims and the aggregation rules applied to them, will find them set out in \citet{TODO_RAG_Eval_Survey}.

Correctness and adherence are computed by program, the first as a comparison against the annotated answer $a_q$ and the second by rule, which is the design principle of the verifiable instructions of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}. Faithfulness and answer relevance are scored by a separate language model, as in \citet{es2023ragas} and \citet{saadfalcon2024ares}.

\subsection{The Judge}

Faithfulness and answer relevance are scored by a separate language model, as \citet{chen2024rgb} do for comparable measures. Since this thesis assumes that language models are sensitive to their instructions, the judge cannot be assumed reliable. Three precautions are taken, following \citet{zheng2023judge}, who document position and verbosity biases in this setting. The judge's agreement with human labelling is measured on about 100 held-out outputs before the main run and reported as Cohen's $\kappa$. Agreement is reported per condition rather than pooled, because the format condition $S_3$ is recognisable from its surface alone and any judge error that varies with condition would bias $\hat{\tau}_{jk}$. The judge's own prompt is fixed, and outputs are shown in random order without condition labels.

\section{Procedure and Analysis}

Every query is run in all 20 cells, three times each, giving $80 \times 20 \times 3 = 4800$ generations. The order of the cells is randomised per query and the timestamp of each generation is recorded, so drift over the collection period can be checked. Each generation is an independent request with caching disabled. Stored for every generation are the query, the condition indices, the context string with the positions of the supporting documents, the output, the number of output tokens, and any document identifiers the system emitted.

Output length is recorded but not held constant. The prompt is one of the things that changes how much the model writes, so removing length would remove part of the effect being measured. \citet{zheng2023judge} additionally report that longer answers are scored more favourably by language-model judges, which is a second reason to record length rather than ignore it.

The analysis is fixed before any data is collected. The main result is the difference in average cost between a prompt condition and the baseline, computed within each context condition:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tauhat}
\hat{\tau}_{jk} = \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{k}) - \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_0, c^{k}),
\qquad
\hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{k}) = \frac{1}{N_Q\,n}\sum_{i=1}^{N_Q}\sum_{r=1}^{n} \ell\bigl(y_{ijkr}\bigr).
\end{equation}

This is the sample version of $\tau_j$ from Equation~\eqref{eq:risk}. The individual metrics are reported as supporting results and read as a breakdown of $\hat{\tau}_{jk}$.

All comparisons are paired at query level. The paired difference in $\ell$ is tested with a paired $t$-test where the normality assumption holds and a Wilcoxon signed-rank test otherwise; binary metrics use McNemar's test. Three families of tests are pre-specified and each is corrected separately by the method of \citet{holm1979}: the effect of the prompt at $c^{\text{ret}}$, the effect of the context at $S_0$, and one interaction contrast asking whether the prompt matters more when the evidence is corrupted,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:interaction}
\delta_j = \bigl[\hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{\text{corr}}) - \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_0, c^{\text{corr}})\bigr] - \bigl[\hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{\text{gold}}) - \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_0, c^{\text{gold}})\bigr].
\end{equation}

One further contrast tests the claim from Chapter 4 that $S_4$, which changes the requirement set, produces a larger effect than $S_1$ through $S_3$, which do not. No other comparison is pre-specified.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

At $N_Q = 80$ and $n = 3$ the design detects within-query shifts of roughly twenty percentage points. It is powered for large effects only. This is enough for the hypothesis under test, since \citet{sclar2024quantifying} report accuracy swings of up to 76 points from formatting changes alone, but a null result may only be reported as the absence of a large effect.

Public datasets bring their own limit. The generator may have seen the questions and their annotated answers during pretraining, so a correct output need not indicate that the retrieved evidence was used. The $\pi_i$ screen of Section~\ref{sec:corpus} bounds this by identifying the queries the model can already answer with no context, and the restricted analysis excludes them, but $\pi_i$ is an imperfect proxy: a model may hold a memorised fact without reproducing it under $c^{\varnothing}$ in three attempts. A second limit follows from the open world. Because $\Phi$ holds only the annotated answers and the facts stated by the indexed passages, requirement 2 is checked over the claims bearing on $a_q$ and not over every claim an output makes, so the accuracy figures bound rather than measure the full correctness of an answer.

Two further limits are noted. The judge may make errors that vary with the condition, which the per-condition agreement check bounds but does not remove; \citet{zheng2023judge} report that such biases persist even under blinding. And the generator parameters $\theta$ are fixed at one model, so every result is conditional on that model. \citet{salinas2024butterfly} find that prompt sensitivity differs between models, so repeating the design on a second model is left to future work.

